A||N||3||helmet1978||How to remove V6 Oil Pressure sending unit||||5||
Z||000000||helmet1978||11-16-2001||04:34 PM||||Hello, <P>I guess my Oil Pressure sending unit has died. Fuel pump keeps running after turning car off. <P>I went to replace it and it seems to be stuck! I removed the battery, and the battery shield but can't get it out. Tried using the large hex surface on top and also the smaller metal one underneath but it just wont budge. Any other suggestions on getting this thing off?<P>Thanks<P>------------------<BR>Paul Heindselman<BR>Southwest Idaho Fiero Club<BR>1986 Fiero SE 2M6 - Red<BR>1986 Fiero SE 2M6 - Black (For Sale)<BR>2000 Grand Am GT Sedan - White||||reg||5
Z||000001||Voytek||11-16-2001||06:22 PM||||Not really.  I did mine a few weeks ago.  Yeah, it was a bit tough but gave in after a bit.  No magic.  I'd spray some WD40 and wait a day or so, then try again.<BR>Good luck.<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/voyteks_sig2.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000002||ToddAsh||11-16-2001||06:27 PM|||| I just changed mine this week because my gauge was jumping around.Put a wrench on the base and it came right off.Warm up the motor a little first.Just wondering what does the oil sending unit have to do with the fuel pump running on ? HaveFun,Todd||||reg||1
Z||000003||Old Lar||11-16-2001||08:32 PM||||Fuel pump and oil sending unit are on the same circuit. Oil sending unit say there is oil pressure, fuel pump runs to start car.  No fuel pressure, no fuel pump.<P>When the oil sender unit fails, it pegs the oil guage so that the fuel pump will continue to operate and you can drive.<P>My simplified definition after three sending units replaced on each of two Fieros.||||reg||1
